{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q014_paper_example_reclassification_pa1492","answer":"In SCOP 1.69, PA1492 from 1t1j and nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase from 1f8y were only in the same top SCOP class, c, Alpha and beta proteins (a/b). 1t1j chains A and B were domains d1t1ja_ and d1t1jb_ with sunids 106249 and 106250, classified as c.137.1.1: fold c.137, Hypothetical protein PA1492; superfamily c.137.1, Hypothetical protein PA1492; family c.137.1.1, Hypothetical protein PA1492. 1f8y chains A and B were d1f8ya_ and d1f8yb_ with sunids 31395 and 31396, classified separately as c.23.14.1: fold c.23, Flavodoxin-like; superfamily c.23.14, N-deoxyribosyltransferase; family c.23.14.1, N-deoxyribosyltransferase. Thus SCOP 1.69 did not encode a fold-, superfamily-, or family-level evolutionary relationship between PA1492 and 1f8y; they merely shared the broad a/b structural class.

In the current SCOPe release reported by the API, 2.08, PA1492 has been reparented into the nucleoside/deoxyribosyltransferase-like lineage while remaining a distinct family. The real 1t1j protein domains are d1t1ja1, 1t1j A:1-118, and d1t1jb1, 1t1j B:1-118, still with sunids 106249 and 106250, but now with SCCS c.23.14.2 under fold c.23, Flavodoxin-like; superfamily c.23.14, N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like; family c.23.14.2, Hypothetical protein PA1492. 1f8y remains c.23.14.1, N-deoxyribosyltransferase, under the same c.23.14 superfamily. SCOPe also lists tiny 1t1j tag fragments d1t1ja2 and d1t1jb2 as l.1.1.1 artifacts; those are not the PA1492 domains relevant to Andreeva et al.'s reclassification example.

So the relationship changed from “different folds and different superfamilies in the same a/b class” in SCOP 1.69 to “same Flavodoxin-like fold and same N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like superfamily, but different families” in SCOPe 2.08. The current placement is superfamily-level evidence for remote common ancestry and makes 1f8y a same-superfamily structural template for the PA1492 core fold, but it still stops short of assigning PA1492 to the 1f8y N-deoxyribosyltransferase family or safely transferring the exact nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase function without additional biochemical evidence.","evidence_used":["/api/v1","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1t1j","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1f8y","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1t1j","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1f8y","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1t1j/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1f8y/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1t1j/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1f8y/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja_","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1f8ya_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1t1ja1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1f8ya_","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/110463","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/52312","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110463","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52312","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/110462","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/52311","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110462","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52311","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/110461","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/52310","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110461","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52310","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/110460","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/52309","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52309","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/110459","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/52171","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52171","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sunids/51349","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51349"]}]}
